Step 1: Prep the Jetson
First, boot up your Jetson Orin Nano, connect it to your Wi-Fi, and open a terminal.
Clone your repository (or copy your backend folder) onto the Jetson.

Navigate into the backend folder:

Bash
cd path/to/BHARATI/backend
Step 2: Install Dependencies
Since this is a fresh environment, you need to install the Python libraries. The Jetson has an NVIDIA GPU, so it will leverage TensorRT or standard CUDA to run YOLO incredibly fast.

Run this in your terminal:

Bash
pip3 install fastapi uvicorn ultralytics pymongo twilio opencv-python numpy pydantic
(Note: Because the Jetson runs on ARM architecture, if it struggles to install ultralytics or torch via standard pip, you may need to install the specific PyTorch wheel provided by NVIDIA for your version of JetPack. Let me know if it throws a PyTorch error here).

Step 3: Start the FastAPI Server
Once the libraries are installed, start your Uvicorn server exactly like you would on your laptop.

Bash
python3 -m uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
Your backend is now running locally on the Jetson! You can verify this by opening a browser on the Jetson and going to http://localhost:8000/status.

Step 4: Create the Public Tunnel (Ngrok)
Right now, your Vercel frontend (which lives on the public internet) cannot see your Jetson (which lives on your private Wi-Fi). We will use a free tool called Ngrok to punch a secure hole through your router.

Keep your Uvicorn server running. Open a second terminal window on the Jetson.

Install Ngrok:

Bash
curl -s https://ngrok-agent.s3.amazonaws.com/ngrok.asc | sudo tee /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/ngrok.asc >/dev/null
echo "deb https://ngrok-agent.s3.amazonaws.com buster main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ngrok.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install ngrok
Go to ngrok.com, create a free account, and grab your Authtoken from the dashboard.  

Authenticate your Jetson:  

Bash
ngrok config add-authtoken YOUR_AUTH_TOKEN
Start the tunnel, pointing it at your FastAPI port:  

Bash
ngrok http 8000
Ngrok will clear the terminal and output a screen with a Forwarding URL that looks something like [https://a1b2-c3d4.ngrok-free.app](https://a1b2-c3d4.ngrok-free.app).  

Step 5: Connect Vercel to the Jetson
That Ngrok URL is your Jetson's new, temporary public address!

On your laptop, open src/App.js.

Update line 17 with the new Ngrok URL:

JavaScript
const API_URL = "https://a1b2-c3d4.ngrok-free.app"; // Use your exact Ngrok URL
Save, commit, and push this to GitHub so Vercel rebuilds the frontend.